  {
    "episode_id": 73962,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73962.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 41,
    "turn_text": "First of all, I think the most important reality in Ukraine is that Putin will not lose that war. It's a fantasy you think that Ukraine can win. They can't lob missiles into Moscow to try and reduce the Russian support for the war. Losing is existential to Putin. He has nuclear weapons. He would use them if they ever came to that. So you have to recognize that reality. I don't like the reality. He's a war criminal. I appreciate what the courageous Ukrainian people have done to fight for their freedom and their families. But the fact of the matter is, the only way that war ends is with the negotiated settlement. Every day that goes by, more Ukrainians die, more Russian conscripts die. I think you'll join that. And more of Ukraine gets destroyed. So the strategy of the United States and Ukraine ought to be to try and pressure Putin into actually settling. Stop talking about winning this war, driving Russia out of Crimea and Eastern Ukraine. It's not going to happen. So let's continue that when he took office. I was there for his inauguration. I visited them two months later. At that point in time, he wanted to do a peace deal with Putin, recognizing it may not be popular, but he would not be able to push Putin out of Crimea or Eastern Ukraine. Here we are, three years later, and more of Ukraine's destroyed. Russia invaded. Again, war crimes committed. I don't like that. I don't know how you're going to hold Putin accountable for that. Again, he has nuclear weapons. He's the head of the state. He's the head of Russia. You have to recognize that reality and try and bring this war to an end.",
